WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:07.952 --> 00:00:08.786
&gt;&gt;&gt; Don't look now, but there's

00:00:08.919 --> 00:00:10.821
something funny going on over

00:00:10.955 --> 00:00:11.956
there at the bank, George.

00:00:11.989 --> 00:00:13.624
&gt;&gt; I've never really seen one.

00:00:13.958 --> 00:00:15.025
That's got all the earmarks of

00:00:15.159 --> 00:00:15.459
being a run.

00:00:15.593 --> 00:00:17.962
&gt;&gt; The classic scene from it's

00:00:17.962 --> 00:00:18.829
a wonderful life, dramatizes

00:00:18.963 --> 00:00:21.966
the panic involved in a run on

00:00:21.966 --> 00:00:22.032
the bank.

00:00:22.166 --> 00:00:23.500
It happens when there's a loss

00:00:23.634 --> 00:00:25.369
of confidence in the bank, and

00:00:26.337 --> 00:00:27.004
that leads to large

00:00:27.137 --> 00:00:27.438
withdrawals.

00:00:27.571 --> 00:00:28.939
The bank can't pay everyone who

00:00:29.073 --> 00:00:30.774
wants their money all at once,

00:00:30.941 --> 00:00:31.976
and it fails.

00:00:31.976 --> 00:00:33.978
During the great depression,

00:00:33.978 --> 00:00:36.413
thousands of banks failed.

00:00:36.547 --> 00:00:36.947
9,000 to be exact.

00:00:37.081 --> 00:00:38.649
Taking $7 billion worth of

00:00:38.782 --> 00:00:39.984
customers assets with them.

00:00:39.984 --> 00:00:42.419
That's why the new deal

00:00:42.553 --> 00:00:43.988
included laws to ensure

00:00:43.988 --> 00:00:47.224
deposits and boost confidence

00:00:47.358 --> 00:00:48.258
in financial institutions.

00:00:48.392 --> 00:00:50.194
The federal deposit insurance

00:00:50.961 --> 00:00:52.229
corporation, the fdic, was

00:00:52.529 --> 00:00:52.896
created in 1933.

00:00:53.030 --> 00:00:59.269
And today covers up to $250,000

00:00:59.403 --> 00:01:00.871
per depositor at fdic-insured

00:01:01.005 --> 00:01:01.205
banks.

00:01:01.338 --> 00:01:02.006
Actual bank failures still

00:01:02.072 --> 00:01:02.272
happen.

00:01:02.406 --> 00:01:04.008
There were a lot during the

00:01:04.008 --> 00:01:05.442
great financial crisis in 2008.

00:01:05.576 --> 00:01:08.779
But in recent years, just a

00:01:08.912 --> 00:01:10.180
handful every year.

00:01:10.314 --> 00:01:11.515
And none in 2021 and 2022.

00:01:11.649 --> 00:01:13.283
The danger with a bank run is

00:01:13.550 --> 00:01:16.353
that panic can spread to other

00:01:16.487 --> 00:01:16.687
banks.

00:01:16.820 --> 00:01:17.287
It's called contagion.

00:01:17.421 --> 00:01:20.257
&gt;&gt; Day one, there were two

00:01:20.391 --> 00:01:20.591
people.

00:01:20.724 --> 00:01:21.225
And then four.

00:01:21.458 --> 00:01:22.026
And then 16.

00:01:22.026 --> 00:01:24.028
In 3 months, it's a billion.

00:01:24.028 --> 00:01:24.762
That's where we're headed.

00:01:24.895 --> 00:01:26.797
&gt;&gt; So what starts as a problem

00:01:26.930 --> 00:01:28.432
at a single bank can quickly

00:01:28.565 --> 00:01:30.668
turn into a crisis for the

00:01:30.801 --> 00:01:31.135
entire system.

00:01:31.268 --> 00:01:34.038
That's what happened during the

00:01:34.071 --> 00:01:35.606
great financial crisis in 2008,

00:01:36.040 --> 00:01:37.441
and it's why the collapse of

00:01:37.708 --> 00:01:40.044
silicon valley bank, the second

00:01:40.044 --> 00:01:41.011
largest U.S. Bank failure of

00:01:41.145 --> 00:01:42.746
all time, has Wall Street on

00:01:42.880 --> 00:01:43.047
edge.

00:01:43.180 --> 00:01:46.650
&gt;&gt; A look at how this all

00:01:46.784 --> 00:01:47.117
actually works.

00:01:47.251 --> 00:01:50.087
When you have a run on a bank,

00:01:50.220 --> 00:01:52.356
how do you prevent future bank

00:01:52.489 --> 00:01:52.723
failures?

00:01:52.856 --> 00:01:54.525
Is the government doing too

00:01:54.658 --> 00:01:55.025
much, too little?

00:01:55.159 --> 00:01:56.060
We also invited the white house

00:01:56.060 --> 00:01:58.062
to join us tonight on the

00:01:58.062 --> 00:02:00.531
program to help answer American

00:02:00.664 --> 00:02:00.998
questions.

00:02:01.131 --> 00:02:02.599
They declined that invitation,

00:02:02.733 --> 00:02:05.135
but let's bring in Larry

00:02:05.269 --> 00:02:06.070
Summers for his insight.

00:02:06.103 --> 00:02:06.670
Secretary Summers, thank you

00:02:06.804 --> 00:02:08.105
very much for being with us

00:02:08.238 --> 00:02:08.472
tonight.

00:02:08.605 --> 00:02:10.340
And I just want to begin with

00:02:10.474 --> 00:02:11.008
your level of concern.

00:02:11.141 --> 00:02:13.711
I mean, we have more banks

00:02:14.078 --> 00:02:16.246
tonight on the brink.

00:02:16.380 --> 00:02:19.316
What do the American people

00:02:19.616 --> 00:02:21.251
need to be preparing for?

00:02:21.385 --> 00:02:26.090
&gt;&gt; I don't think this is a time

00:02:26.156 --> 00:02:28.092
for panic or alarm.

00:02:28.158 --> 00:02:29.693
This is not 2008 where people

00:02:30.094 --> 00:02:31.428
needed to be worried about

00:02:31.562 --> 00:02:34.465
whether they could get their

00:02:34.598 --> 00:02:39.470
money from the atm machine.

00:02:39.603 --> 00:02:41.472
It absolutely is not that.

00:02:41.605 --> 00:02:45.175
There may well be concerns at

00:02:45.476 --> 00:02:47.644
certain banks, and they may

00:02:47.778 --> 00:02:50.114
even be more bank closures.

00:02:50.114 --> 00:02:51.115
But the government's made it

00:02:51.248 --> 00:02:54.251
clear that it is determined to

00:02:54.384 --> 00:02:56.286
ensure that all depositors will

00:02:56.420 --> 00:03:00.124
be able to get their money

00:03:00.190 --> 00:03:01.325
promptly and in full.

00:03:01.458 --> 00:03:04.661
That's what it signaled with

00:03:04.795 --> 00:03:07.631
its actions at svb, the silicon

00:03:07.765 --> 00:03:08.132
valley bank.

00:03:08.198 --> 00:03:10.134
And signature bank and with the

00:03:10.134 --> 00:03:14.538
support that it provided to the

00:03:14.671 --> 00:03:16.206
banking system more broadly.

00:03:16.340 --> 00:03:19.243
It's not just people with

00:03:19.376 --> 00:03:20.144
insured deposits under $250,000

00:03:20.144 --> 00:03:22.212
who will be able to get their

00:03:22.346 --> 00:03:22.613
money back.

00:03:22.746 --> 00:03:26.283
It's people with money over

00:03:26.416 --> 00:03:26.650
$250,000.

00:03:26.784 --> 00:03:27.017
&gt;&gt; Right.

00:03:27.151 --> 00:03:29.753
&gt;&gt; So there are real issues

00:03:30.154 --> 00:03:30.354
here.

00:03:30.354 --> 00:03:32.156
There's major failures of

00:03:32.156 --> 00:03:33.757
financial regulation, but this

00:03:34.158 --> 00:03:36.794
should not be something that

00:03:36.927 --> 00:03:38.328
causes panic or consternation

00:03:38.462 --> 00:03:39.463
or fear among your viewers.

00:03:39.596 --> 00:03:45.102
&gt;&gt; You know, it is so

00:03:45.269 --> 00:03:45.702
significant, though.

00:03:45.836 --> 00:03:48.739
Roger Altman who worked in the

00:03:48.872 --> 00:03:51.108
treasury, he called to us this

00:03:51.241 --> 00:03:52.176
week breathtaking what the

00:03:52.309 --> 00:03:53.043
government has done here,

00:03:53.177 --> 00:03:54.545
stepping in, in this way.

00:03:54.678 --> 00:03:55.813
Now the question becomes,

00:03:56.180 --> 00:03:58.248
secretary, what does the fed

00:03:58.382 --> 00:03:58.515
do?

00:03:58.649 --> 00:04:01.118
What does the fed do next week

00:04:01.251 --> 00:04:01.819
on interest rates?

00:04:02.186 --> 00:04:05.722
Given their dual mandate is

00:04:05.856 --> 00:04:06.423
maximum employment and price-

00:04:06.557 --> 00:04:07.157
ability, but they know whatever

00:04:07.291 --> 00:04:11.628
they do if they raise rates

00:04:11.762 --> 00:04:14.198
will really impact bank

00:04:14.198 --> 00:04:16.200
stability right now.

00:04:16.200 --> 00:04:20.204
&gt;&gt; Look, I think the fed has no

00:04:20.204 --> 00:04:23.273
choice but to remain focused on

00:04:23.407 --> 00:04:23.841
the inflation issue.

00:04:24.208 --> 00:04:26.210
If inflation goes up and

00:04:26.210 --> 00:04:27.010
inflation is not controlled,

00:04:27.144 --> 00:04:29.680
we're going to have higher

00:04:29.847 --> 00:04:30.514
interest rates, and higher

00:04:30.647 --> 00:04:32.349
interest rates are going to

00:04:32.482 --> 00:04:33.684
make these problems more

00:04:33.817 --> 00:04:34.218
serious.

00:04:34.218 --> 00:04:35.385
The judgment the fed's going to

00:04:35.519 --> 00:04:38.488
have to make is that because of

00:04:38.622 --> 00:04:39.223
what's happened, there's surely

00:04:39.356 --> 00:04:41.558
going to be some reductions in

00:04:42.326 --> 00:04:44.228
the supply of credit, and so in

00:04:44.228 --> 00:04:47.364
a sense without the fed raising

00:04:47.497 --> 00:04:49.499
interest rates, monetary

00:04:49.633 --> 00:04:50.234
conditions have tightened.

00:04:50.300 --> 00:04:52.236
And the question the fed's

00:04:52.236 --> 00:04:56.540
going to have to ask itself is

00:04:56.673 --> 00:04:57.875
have monetary conditions

00:04:58.242 --> 00:05:01.345
tightened sufficiently that

00:05:01.478 --> 00:05:03.046
we're responding to the

00:05:03.180 --> 00:05:04.481
inflation threat without any

00:05:04.615 --> 00:05:07.251
further rate increases or

00:05:07.417 --> 00:05:08.752
without a further rate increase

00:05:08.886 --> 00:05:09.152
this month?

00:05:09.286 --> 00:05:12.322
And I don't think we can know

00:05:12.456 --> 00:05:15.092
the answer to that question

00:05:15.225 --> 00:05:15.659
with confidence.

00:05:15.792 --> 00:05:17.127
And every day we're getting new

00:05:17.394 --> 00:05:18.996
information as we see what

00:05:19.129 --> 00:05:23.233
happens to banks, as we see how

00:05:23.367 --> 00:05:26.403
businesses are responding to

00:05:26.536 --> 00:05:27.070
these developments.

00:05:27.204 --> 00:05:30.274
Certainly the 50 basis points

00:05:30.507 --> 00:05:31.608
that I and others thought was a

00:05:31.742 --> 00:05:34.278
viable option before all of

00:05:34.278 --> 00:05:35.145
this financial difficulty, that

00:05:35.279 --> 00:05:37.781
has to be off the table.

00:05:37.915 --> 00:05:40.651
But I would be concerned if the

00:05:40.784 --> 00:05:43.120
fed were to stop all together

00:05:43.253 --> 00:05:44.855
raising interest rates, that it

00:05:44.988 --> 00:05:46.290
would be sending a signal that

00:05:46.290 --> 00:05:47.925
it was quite alarmed about the

00:05:48.292 --> 00:05:50.294
economy, and people would feel

00:05:50.294 --> 00:05:52.629
that if the fed was alarmed,

00:05:52.763 --> 00:05:54.364
they should be alarmed.

00:05:54.498 --> 00:05:56.733
And it could be

00:05:56.867 --> 00:05:57.267
counterproductive.

00:05:57.401 --> 00:06:01.305
So I don't have a clear view

00:06:01.438 --> 00:06:01.705
right now.

00:06:01.838 --> 00:06:02.839
My instinct is if they can find

00:06:02.973 --> 00:06:06.410
a way to safely raise rates by

00:06:06.543 --> 00:06:07.844
25 basis points, that's

00:06:07.978 --> 00:06:09.947
probably the better thing to be

00:06:10.314 --> 00:06:11.848
looking for, but that's not a

00:06:11.982 --> 00:06:12.549
judgment that one can

00:06:12.683 --> 00:06:13.517
confidently make right now.

00:06:13.650 --> 00:06:14.451
&gt;&gt; That's a great point.

00:06:14.584 --> 00:06:16.320
I mean, they sort of have to

00:06:16.420 --> 00:06:21.358
get it right like a goldilocks

00:06:21.491 --> 00:06:21.825
decision here.

00:06:21.959 --> 00:06:23.560
The former head of the fdic

00:06:23.760 --> 00:06:26.330
said the fed should pause

00:06:26.496 --> 00:06:27.397
completely on raising rates.

00:06:27.531 --> 00:06:28.332
Summers is saying you're going

00:06:28.565 --> 00:06:28.899
to the markets.

00:06:29.032 --> 00:06:29.967
You have a question.

00:06:30.334 --> 00:06:32.369
&gt;&gt; She's not the only one who

00:06:32.502 --> 00:06:34.504
said they should pause all

00:06:34.638 --> 00:06:34.871
together.

00:06:35.005 --> 00:06:36.340
A week ago, inflation was still

00:06:36.373 --> 00:06:39.109
issue number one for the

00:06:39.242 --> 00:06:39.743
typical American family.

00:06:39.876 --> 00:06:40.744
Tonight, issue number one for

00:06:40.877 --> 00:06:42.346
the typical American family if

00:06:42.346 --> 00:06:43.613
you look at Google search

00:06:43.747 --> 00:06:45.615
trends is, is my money safe?

00:06:45.749 --> 00:06:47.050
Should I move my money?

00:06:47.184 --> 00:06:49.853
A lot has changed in the past

00:06:49.987 --> 00:06:50.354
week.

00:06:50.354 --> 00:06:51.254
Talk to us, secretary Summers,

00:06:51.388 --> 00:06:53.090
about the inflation fight and

00:06:53.223 --> 00:06:55.225
how that should still be in the

00:06:55.359 --> 00:06:58.395
forefront of what the fed is

00:06:58.528 --> 00:07:01.932
trying to do in your view.

00:07:02.366 --> 00:07:03.600
&gt;&gt; Look, Americans' money is

00:07:03.734 --> 00:07:03.900
safe.

00:07:04.034 --> 00:07:05.335
Americans' money will be safe

00:07:05.469 --> 00:07:08.372
if they don't raise rates at

00:07:08.372 --> 00:07:08.405
all.

00:07:08.538 --> 00:07:10.374
Americans' money will be safe

00:07:10.707 --> 00:07:12.876
if they do raise rates by 25

00:07:13.010 --> 00:07:14.011
basis points because the

00:07:14.945 --> 00:07:18.515
government has indicated that

00:07:18.648 --> 00:07:19.950
it's standing behind bank

00:07:20.384 --> 00:07:21.051
deposits.

00:07:21.051 --> 00:07:23.086
And as that message is repeated

00:07:23.220 --> 00:07:24.388
and becomes clear, as people

00:07:24.454 --> 00:07:26.390
see that those who have

00:07:26.456 --> 00:07:28.692
deposited money even in the

00:07:28.825 --> 00:07:30.394
banks that failed, and even the

00:07:30.394 --> 00:07:32.796
people who were not insured are

00:07:32.929 --> 00:07:35.032
getting their money back in

00:07:35.165 --> 00:07:35.332
full.

00:07:35.465 --> 00:07:38.402
But we are still also seeing

00:07:38.535 --> 00:07:40.404
very substantial rates of

00:07:40.404 --> 00:07:40.670
inflation.

00:07:40.804 --> 00:07:44.007
And if those substantial rates

00:07:44.408 --> 00:07:46.476
of inflation continue they will

00:07:46.610 --> 00:07:47.711
erode people's purchasing power

00:07:47.844 --> 00:07:49.112
and their ability to spend.

00:07:49.246 --> 00:07:50.981
They will be incorporated in

00:07:51.114 --> 00:07:54.418
yet higher interest rates,

00:07:54.484 --> 00:07:57.287
which will mean further

00:07:57.421 --> 00:07:57.821
financial strains.

00:07:57.954 --> 00:08:00.857
So I think the fed has to

00:08:00.991 --> 00:08:03.627
tiptoe very carefully through

00:08:03.760 --> 00:08:08.498
what is certainly a mine field

00:08:09.699 --> 00:08:10.434
at this point.

00:08:10.434 --> 00:08:13.103
But again, inflation is a real

00:08:13.236 --> 00:08:14.104
threat, not being able to get

00:08:14.438 --> 00:08:17.174
your money out of the bank is a

00:08:17.307 --> 00:08:21.745
fear, but it is not a realistic

00:08:21.878 --> 00:08:24.681
possibility or realistic threat

00:08:24.815 --> 00:08:26.450
that Americans are facing.

00:08:26.583 --> 00:08:26.817
&gt;&gt; Phil?

00:08:26.950 --> 00:08:28.452
&gt;&gt; One of the things I've been

00:08:28.452 --> 00:08:29.653
trying to figure out through

00:08:29.786 --> 00:08:32.456
the course of this week,

00:08:32.522 --> 00:08:33.390
speaking personally, was

00:08:33.523 --> 00:08:34.458
stunned by the scale and the

00:08:34.558 --> 00:08:40.964
speed by which the government

00:08:41.098 --> 00:08:41.565
reacted and coalesced.

00:08:41.698 --> 00:08:43.633
Using 133 for the fed facility,

00:08:43.767 --> 00:08:46.870
what else do they have in the

00:08:47.003 --> 00:08:49.339
chamber to use a bad analogy.

00:08:49.473 --> 00:08:50.841
When you're thinking about what

00:08:50.974 --> 00:08:52.876
might happen next, if something

00:08:53.009 --> 00:08:56.480
happens next, what else can

00:08:56.613 --> 00:08:57.114
they do?

00:08:57.247 --> 00:08:59.549
That should give people

00:08:59.683 --> 00:09:01.885
confidence that there's more

00:09:02.018 --> 00:09:02.486
left.

00:09:02.486 --> 00:09:05.755
&gt;&gt; They have a huge, they have

00:09:05.889 --> 00:09:07.624
done a huge thing.

00:09:07.757 --> 00:09:10.494
It is a major source, if

00:09:10.560 --> 00:09:13.697
somebody was prepared to loan

00:09:13.830 --> 00:09:15.198
money against a $300,000 house,

00:09:15.332 --> 00:09:19.002
they were prepared to loan

00:09:19.136 --> 00:09:19.369
$400,000.

00:09:19.503 --> 00:09:22.873
That would be a major source of

00:09:23.006 --> 00:09:23.240
support.

00:09:23.373 --> 00:09:24.508
And that's, in effect, what the

00:09:24.508 --> 00:09:26.943
government has done by saying

00:09:27.077 --> 00:09:28.512
it's going to lend against all

00:09:28.512 --> 00:09:30.747
these bonds at par, not against

00:09:30.881 --> 00:09:31.414
their current market value.

00:09:31.548 --> 00:09:33.083
So what the government is going

00:09:33.216 --> 00:09:33.917
to, the main thing the

00:09:34.084 --> 00:09:37.787
government is going to do is

00:09:37.921 --> 00:09:40.524
show that it is money good

00:09:40.524 --> 00:09:44.694
behind its words, and I think

00:09:44.828 --> 00:09:50.033
that will be reassuring on the

00:09:50.167 --> 00:09:51.635
question of deposits.

00:09:51.768 --> 00:09:53.803
Ultimately, one could imagine

00:09:53.937 --> 00:09:56.139
extending an fdic guarantee in

00:09:56.540 --> 00:09:59.843
a formal way to all deposits as

00:10:00.010 --> 00:10:02.746
a matter of logic.

00:10:02.879 --> 00:10:05.015
I would be very surprised if

00:10:05.148 --> 00:10:06.550
that was necessary at this

00:10:06.550 --> 00:10:06.683
point.

00:10:06.816 --> 00:10:11.354
I don't think the real concerns

00:10:11.488 --> 00:10:13.223
here are about deposits.

00:10:13.356 --> 00:10:13.957
They'll get paid.

00:10:14.090 --> 00:10:16.960
The real concerns here are that

00:10:17.093 --> 00:10:21.965
the banking industry is under

00:10:22.098 --> 00:10:23.900
considerable stress, that they

00:10:24.034 --> 00:10:25.902
were too bold, that they're

00:10:26.036 --> 00:10:29.072
going to be regulated more

00:10:29.206 --> 00:10:32.576
heavily, and how we do that

00:10:32.676 --> 00:10:34.911
without precipitating a credit

00:10:35.045 --> 00:10:37.147
crunch that generates a

00:10:37.280 --> 00:10:40.584
recession, that is the very

00:10:40.917 --> 00:10:42.953
serious concern that we have

00:10:43.086 --> 00:10:43.253
here.

00:10:43.386 --> 00:10:48.592
And so I am much more concerned

00:10:48.592 --> 00:10:50.594
about a continuing, strong flow

